WEBVTT

00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:33.610
First inning! Let's shut them down!

00:01:33.610 --> 00:01:34.530
Yeah!

00:01:37.490 --> 00:01:38.740
Let's see...
Top of the first inning.
Bijoudai Sayama High to bat.

00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:42.490
Is Nishiura really playing as well as Roka said?

00:01:42.490 --> 00:01:42.700
Time to find out.

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:44.450
First batter. Shortstop: Number 6, Kawashima.

00:01:44.450 --> 00:01:45.500
Ready!

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:46.160
First batter. Shortstop: Number 6, Kawashima.

00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:47.080
Play!

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:51.750
Ball!

00:01:51.750 --> 00:01:53.550
He didn't even flinch.

00:01:53.550 --> 00:01:57.590
The first pitch went as predicted.
This is one of the catcher's patterns.

00:01:57.590 --> 00:02:00.340
For batters he's facing for the
first time, he'll start with a ball.

00:02:00.340 --> 00:02:02.890
Well, you could call it a
by-the-books lineup, but...

00:02:02.890 --> 00:02:04.520
It was in a really risky spot.

00:02:04.520 --> 00:02:09.400
I've had the most walks on the team since fall.

00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:11.520
If he knows that, he's probably thinking,

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:13.650
"He didn't even flinch."

00:02:13.650 --> 00:02:17.030
"This batter is calm. It seems
like he's seeing the ball clearly."

00:02:17.030 --> 00:02:19.450
If I'm right, Kawashima likes the inner corner.

00:02:20.110 --> 00:02:23.080
Let's try throwing a slider that curves into a ball.

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:27.750
Ball!

00:02:27.750 --> 00:02:31.330
That was close! I really thought I could hit it.

00:02:31.330 --> 00:02:33.460
But it really was a ball.

00:02:33.460 --> 00:02:38.470
Abe avoids throwing strikes in a batter's favored course, too.

00:02:38.470 --> 00:02:42.720
He shouldn't want to show their finishing fastball to batters with good eyes during the first inning.

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:45.010
All right. The coach agrees.

00:02:45.640 --> 00:02:48.980
At 0-2, Kawashima will watch the ball outside of his favored course.

00:02:48.980 --> 00:02:50.480
I won't show him the fastball.

00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:53.060
A shootball to the outside!

00:02:53.060 --> 00:02:53.730
All right!

00:02:56.570 --> 00:02:57.400
He did it!

00:02:57.400 --> 00:02:59.360
Kou, nice batting.

00:02:59.360 --> 00:03:01.360
All three pitches were right on the money!

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:03.660
All three pitches went as expected.

00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:06.450
It seems that all three pitches went as expected.

00:03:06.450 --> 00:03:07.450
Seriously?

00:03:07.450 --> 00:03:09.040
Was it a coincidence?

00:03:09.040 --> 00:03:11.000
Idiot! That's all three of them.

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:13.710
I've had my doubts until now, but...

00:03:13.710 --> 00:03:16.000
We believe in you, Coach!

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:17.210
Took you long enough!

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:27.390
Out!

00:03:27.390 --> 00:03:28.970
What happened?

00:03:28.970 --> 00:03:29.850
Um...

00:03:29.850 --> 00:03:32.190
I thought it was good, but the ball popped up.

00:03:32.190 --> 00:03:34.480
I think it was the fastball.

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:35.650
All right.

00:03:35.650 --> 00:03:38.190
Before you forget the ball's
course, go do image training!

00:03:38.190 --> 00:03:38.940
Okay!

00:03:38.940 --> 00:03:41.150
Tetsurou couldn't make the ball bounce...

00:03:41.150 --> 00:03:43.360
Does that mean the part about the weird fastball is true too?

00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:48.120
Today's predictions were put together by comparing Tousei's data and Abe's leads.

00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:51.460
The fact that those predictions are right means that during the Tousei game,

00:03:51.460 --> 00:03:56.080
Nishiura had the same data as us and
were able to use it even more effectively.

00:03:56.080 --> 00:03:57.630
With a catcher who collects and analyzes data, and is able to fully utilize it,

00:03:57.630 --> 00:04:00.550
Third batter. Second baseman: Number 4, Yano.

00:04:00.550 --> 00:04:03.180
plus a pitcher who can
realize the catcher's lead,

00:04:03.180 --> 00:04:06.510
it's not just a coincidence that the unprepared Tousei team lost.

00:04:06.510 --> 00:04:09.970
This time around, we're prepared for anything.

00:04:09.970 --> 00:04:12.980
Their beginners' luck is coming to an end!

00:04:12.980 --> 00:04:16.440
The third batter, Yano, is a power hitter who likes the inner corner.

00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:18.440
I want to make him hit an infield grounder here.

00:04:18.440 --> 00:04:21.440
Let's see. I can't hit the inside corner.

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:24.070
Ball!

00:04:24.070 --> 00:04:26.870
I see. If I hit it, it'll be a
foul or an infield grounder.

00:04:26.870 --> 00:04:27.530
Why are they all watching it pass?

00:04:27.530 --> 00:04:28.580
Nice pitch!

00:04:28.580 --> 00:04:29.280
Pitch to contact.

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:31.750
Even though they have good eyes, it's somehow...

00:04:31.750 --> 00:04:34.040
There's too little hesitation when they let the ball pass!

00:04:34.830 --> 00:04:38.630
The 100 km I trained my eyes for
yesterday fits the pitch speed perfectly.

00:04:38.630 --> 00:04:40.420
I'll be all over the shootballs, too.

00:04:40.420 --> 00:04:42.760
The shootballs in the video were...

00:04:42.760 --> 00:04:44.380
All right, I should be good.

00:04:44.380 --> 00:04:47.140
A shootball to the outside.

00:04:47.140 --> 00:04:48.100
Just inside!

00:04:48.760 --> 00:04:49.390
He stepped forward?

00:04:49.390 --> 00:04:50.850
Keep going! Keep going!

00:04:50.850 --> 00:04:51.680
Will it go out?

00:04:51.680 --> 00:04:53.100
Mizutani, don't jump! It's fair!

00:04:53.770 --> 00:04:54.810
Fair! Fair!

00:04:54.810 --> 00:04:55.520
Third!

00:04:58.860 --> 00:04:59.940
One run!

00:04:59.940 --> 00:05:04.530
In the fertile green fields, the light shines...

00:05:04.530 --> 00:05:08.780
He extended the bat without hesitation,
as if he knew we would go to the outside.

00:05:09.780 --> 00:05:11.290
One run, already.

00:05:11.290 --> 00:05:13.160
Mihashi! One out!

00:05:13.950 --> 00:05:15.500
O-One out!

00:05:15.500 --> 00:05:18.000
Turn things around! There weren't any mistakes on our side!

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:19.710
The next batter, Wada, has the most runs via hits.

00:05:19.710 --> 00:05:20.880
Fourth batter. Center fielder: Number 8, Wada.

00:05:20.880 --> 00:05:24.300
Even if we walk him, I think it's a good idea to check if he's really aiming for a shootball or not.

00:05:24.300 --> 00:05:26.800
I'm usually best at hitting from the outside course.

00:05:26.800 --> 00:05:30.890
Well, that's not really true, but all I've been hitting in this tournament are outside balls,

00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:35.480
which means that a ball will come to the outside.

00:05:35.480 --> 00:05:38.810
A slider to the inside that'll curve into a ball.

00:05:38.810 --> 00:05:41.060
If it's inside, it'll be a slider.

00:05:41.060 --> 00:05:43.650
If the course is iffy, it'll become a ball, so I'll let it pass.

00:05:44.190 --> 00:05:45.190
To the inside.

00:05:45.820 --> 00:05:46.450
Ball!

00:05:47.110 --> 00:05:49.740
He let it pass. It's the same as the others.

00:05:50.990 --> 00:05:52.740
Have they discovered the pitch types?

00:05:52.740 --> 00:05:54.580
If so, then is it me? Or Mihashi?

00:05:55.200 --> 00:05:57.040
Should I mix in a curve? No, wait.

00:05:57.040 --> 00:05:59.540
If it's a shoot, they'll hit any ball, so...

00:05:59.540 --> 00:06:02.500
If it's outside, let it pass. If it's inside, it's a slider.

00:06:02.500 --> 00:06:05.210
A slider to the lower inside.

00:06:05.210 --> 00:06:07.260
If he lets this pass the same way,

00:06:07.260 --> 00:06:09.180
I'll put the pitches together again with the slider as the axis.

00:06:09.760 --> 00:06:11.050
On the edge...

00:06:11.050 --> 00:06:11.600
Inside!

00:06:17.560 --> 00:06:19.770
A two-run... home run.

00:06:22.020 --> 00:06:27.280
In the fertile green fields, the light shines...

00:06:27.280 --> 00:06:28.700
Mihashi!

00:06:28.700 --> 00:06:30.240
Are you okay?

00:06:30.950 --> 00:06:32.490
Give me your hand.

00:06:33.990 --> 00:06:35.200
Do you understand?

00:06:35.200 --> 00:06:36.950
That one was my fault.

00:06:36.950 --> 00:06:39.120
Even though you're the only one
who gets the blame for their runs...

00:06:39.120 --> 00:06:40.120
I'm sorry.

00:06:41.170 --> 00:06:44.290
We're going to change it up and use the fastball from the start.

00:06:44.290 --> 00:06:45.630
Don't act suspiciously on the mound!

00:06:46.300 --> 00:06:47.130
Yeah!

00:06:47.130 --> 00:06:48.090
Mihashi!

00:06:48.090 --> 00:06:49.550
Go at your own pace, okay!?

00:06:49.550 --> 00:06:50.300
Yeah.

00:06:51.220 --> 00:06:51.680
Abe's hand was basically as cold as mine.

00:06:51.680 --> 00:06:56.180
Fifth batter. Right fielder: Number 9, Miyata.

00:06:56.180 --> 00:06:57.180
Play!

00:06:58.640 --> 00:06:59.520
Fastball.

00:06:59.520 --> 00:07:00.060
Inside!

00:07:02.810 --> 00:07:03.690
Out!

00:07:03.690 --> 00:07:05.860
It wasn't a shootball.

00:07:05.860 --> 00:07:07.980
Wasn't that a fastball just now?

00:07:07.980 --> 00:07:09.950
Sixth batter. Left fielder: Number 7, Kitamura.

00:07:09.950 --> 00:07:10.900
Coach!

00:07:10.900 --> 00:07:14.910
The first ball I hit went inside, but it wasn't a shootball...

00:07:14.910 --> 00:07:16.080
Got it!

00:07:16.950 --> 00:07:17.870
Out!

00:07:17.870 --> 00:07:19.080
Three runs, huh?

00:07:19.080 --> 00:07:21.330
All right, let's go defend!

00:07:21.330 --> 00:07:23.420
Don't let my two runs go to waste!

00:07:23.420 --> 00:07:24.330
Yeah!

00:07:25.340 --> 00:07:26.420
Let's do "that."

00:07:26.420 --> 00:07:27.130
Okay.

00:07:27.130 --> 00:07:27.920
Mihashi!

00:07:27.920 --> 00:07:29.630
We're doing "that."

00:07:29.630 --> 00:07:30.840
Rock, paper, scissors!

00:07:30.840 --> 00:07:33.890
Scissors! Scissors! Scissors! Scissors!

00:07:36.220 --> 00:07:38.470
Hey! What are you doing!?

00:07:38.470 --> 00:07:39.350
Done!

00:07:39.350 --> 00:07:41.560
It's a way to instantly relax.

00:07:42.390 --> 00:07:45.860
Izumi says that the first batter gets nervous, so we came up with it.

00:07:47.570 --> 00:07:48.480
You okay?

00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:49.990
Y-Y-Yeah.

00:07:49.990 --> 00:07:51.190
Are you relaxed?

00:07:51.190 --> 00:07:51.900
Y-Y...

00:07:55.070 --> 00:07:57.240
I kind of feel ticklish too!

00:07:57.240 --> 00:07:59.580
Just looking is enough for me.

00:07:59.580 --> 00:08:00.790
Good afternoon!

00:08:00.790 --> 00:08:03.330
Thank you for coming to cheer in the heat today!

00:08:03.330 --> 00:08:04.040
It's 3-0 right now, but...

00:08:04.040 --> 00:08:05.830
Bottom of the first inning.
Nishiura High School to bat.

00:08:05.830 --> 00:08:09.000
we haven't gone on offense yet,
so it's natural that we haven't scored.

00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:12.510
First batter. Center Fielder: Number 8, Izumi.
The match is just starting,
so we're counting on your cheers!

00:08:12.510 --> 00:08:14.180
All right!

00:08:14.180 --> 00:08:16.850
I'll watch the ball's course until there are two strikes.

00:08:16.850 --> 00:08:19.180
But if it's a good ball, I'll hit it.

00:08:20.970 --> 00:08:22.020
I can make it!

00:08:25.230 --> 00:08:26.690
Shit. They caught it in front?

00:08:26.690 --> 00:08:28.150
One out!
Second batter. Second baseman: Number 4, Sakaeguchi.

00:08:28.150 --> 00:08:31.530
As long as we get Izumi's hits to float,
they won't have the power to pass the infield.

00:08:31.530 --> 00:08:32.320
Ready!

00:08:33.190 --> 00:08:35.490
Now it's the unmoving second batter.

00:08:35.490 --> 00:08:38.160
This guy is practically all bunts,
so we have almost no data on him.

00:08:38.160 --> 00:08:40.120
First, we'll try to test him with power.

00:08:41.330 --> 00:08:42.000
Strike!

00:08:43.120 --> 00:08:46.080
Looking at this, Tousei's speed and angle are both better!

00:08:49.290 --> 00:08:51.170
All right! It all starts here!

00:08:51.170 --> 00:08:53.380
It went a bit too far into the middle.
Third batter. Shortstop: Number 6, Suyama.

00:08:53.380 --> 00:08:55.430
Oh well. We'll come back with this next one.

00:08:55.430 --> 00:08:56.680
Third batter. Shortstop: Number 6, Suyama.

00:08:56.680 --> 00:08:58.430
A bunt?

00:08:58.430 --> 00:08:59.800
How bold.

00:08:59.800 --> 00:09:01.810
Tajima was only able to score a run with a runner on second...

00:09:01.810 --> 00:09:03.680
during the ninth inning of the Tousei game.

00:09:03.680 --> 00:09:04.850
Let him do it.

00:09:11.020 --> 00:09:11.690
Out!

00:09:11.690 --> 00:09:13.030
Fourth batter. Third Baseman: Number 5, Tajima.
Two outs and a runner on second.

00:09:13.030 --> 00:09:14.440
Don't be too impatient and get caught in a change-up.

00:09:14.440 --> 00:09:15.320
We're counting on you!

00:09:15.320 --> 00:09:16.360
Fourth batter. Third Baseman: Number 5, Tajima.

00:09:16.360 --> 00:09:17.280
Ready!

00:09:17.280 --> 00:09:20.450
It seems like this guy is an exceptionally good player,

00:09:20.450 --> 00:09:22.790
but he's about as far from
the mound as the first batter.

00:09:22.790 --> 00:09:25.250
We can go with the same approach.

00:09:25.870 --> 00:09:27.250
The outfielders have all moved in.

00:09:27.830 --> 00:09:30.670
They did the same thing for Izumi's at-bat.

00:09:30.670 --> 00:09:33.960
If this is specifically for us, then they want to make us hit outfield flyballs.

00:09:34.550 --> 00:09:36.050
If so, then he'll come at me...

00:09:36.050 --> 00:09:37.130
up high!

00:09:38.510 --> 00:09:39.840
It bounced!

00:09:39.840 --> 00:09:41.050
He can make it home!

00:09:41.050 --> 00:09:42.850
Sakaeguchi, go home!

00:09:42.850 --> 00:09:44.810
Fourth!

00:09:45.930 --> 00:09:46.890
No cut!

00:09:50.270 --> 00:09:51.190
Out!

00:09:51.190 --> 00:09:52.150
All right!

00:09:52.150 --> 00:09:56.030
No way in hell I'm losing to a co-ed school with cheer girls!

00:09:56.030 --> 00:09:57.320
Zero runs.

00:09:57.320 --> 00:09:59.700
At worst, let's cut it off at the second batter.

00:10:00.700 --> 00:10:01.490
Right.

00:10:01.490 --> 00:10:04.160
I guess we have to use the fastball.

00:10:04.160 --> 00:10:06.870
I have to test out the curve, too,
before it rotates back to the top...

00:10:06.870 --> 00:10:08.040
Abe!

00:10:08.040 --> 00:10:08.750
Yes?

00:10:08.750 --> 00:10:10.500
Shall I give out signs?

00:10:11.670 --> 00:10:12.960
Please, let me do it.

00:10:14.050 --> 00:10:15.550
Something's strange.

00:10:15.550 --> 00:10:18.840
I wonder if Abe's hand is still cold?

00:10:18.840 --> 00:10:21.510
If Abe's no good, I'm no good either!

00:10:21.970 --> 00:10:23.930
We'll get some runs in the next inning.

00:10:23.930 --> 00:10:25.850
First, you have to hold them down here!

00:10:25.850 --> 00:10:28.180
I really can't bring myself to tickle him.

00:10:28.180 --> 00:10:32.690
Th-There's no point in being so dismal.

00:10:32.690 --> 00:10:35.150
We'll score some runs next time.

00:10:35.150 --> 00:10:36.820
I just have to hold them back here.

00:10:37.610 --> 00:10:39.860
I'm sure Abe is fine.

00:10:39.860 --> 00:10:42.160
That means I'm fine too.

00:10:45.620 --> 00:10:47.290
Nice back home!

00:10:47.290 --> 00:10:48.580
Way to show your stuff!

00:10:48.580 --> 00:10:51.830
As a catcher, I can get by with good form and control.

00:10:51.830 --> 00:10:53.960
But in the outfield, your arm speaks for you.

00:10:53.960 --> 00:10:55.170
Quit bragging!

00:10:55.170 --> 00:10:57.800
So you finally understand the outfield's charm?

00:10:57.800 --> 00:11:00.970
He really does have an amazing arm.
I can't compare at all.

00:11:01.630 --> 00:11:03.090
No. Be positive.

00:11:03.090 --> 00:11:05.010
The catcher can't lose confidence!

00:11:05.010 --> 00:11:05.850
Takeshi.

00:11:06.850 --> 00:11:11.230
If you try any more risky plays on the plate, I'll tell the coach to bring you in.

00:11:12.020 --> 00:11:14.400
You remember me saying that in the third round, right?

00:11:14.400 --> 00:11:15.360
Yeah.

00:11:15.360 --> 00:11:19.690
You just might want to win more than anyone else.

00:11:19.690 --> 00:11:22.030
But every time you make a play that just barely adheres to the rules,

00:11:22.030 --> 00:11:23.700
you're smearing mud on all of our faces.

00:11:23.700 --> 00:11:24.950
Top of the second inning.
Bijoudai Sayama High to bat.

00:11:24.950 --> 00:11:26.450
I know.

00:11:26.450 --> 00:11:27.160
Top of the second inning.
Bijoudai Sayama High to bat.

00:11:27.160 --> 00:11:28.580
On your own face, too.

00:11:28.580 --> 00:11:29.580
Seventh batter. First baseman: Number 3, Matsushita.

00:11:29.580 --> 00:11:31.250
Let's win with pride.

00:11:31.250 --> 00:11:33.120
Seventh batter. First baseman: Number 3, Matsushita.

00:11:33.120 --> 00:11:34.000
Ready!

00:11:34.630 --> 00:11:37.340
Matsushita has a tendency to hit outside balls.

00:11:37.340 --> 00:11:40.300
I want to test out a curveball after putting in a slider, but...

00:11:40.970 --> 00:11:42.300
A slider.

00:11:43.220 --> 00:11:43.970
Strike!

00:11:43.970 --> 00:11:45.760
He didn't swing.

00:11:45.760 --> 00:11:47.100
All right. Let's try out a curveball.

00:11:47.890 --> 00:11:48.930
A curveball.

00:11:48.930 --> 00:11:49.640
Inside!

00:11:52.940 --> 00:11:53.850
Out!

00:11:54.310 --> 00:11:55.440
Nice, third!

00:11:55.440 --> 00:11:56.400
Nice pitch!

00:11:56.400 --> 00:12:00.900
Ready!
Eigth batter. Catcher: Number 2, Kurata.

00:12:01.530 --> 00:12:03.700
This person is Kurata.

00:12:03.700 --> 00:12:08.370
This is a mnemonic for Kurata.
Inside a cellar... So he likes the inside corner.

00:12:08.370 --> 00:12:09.990
Low, to the outside.

00:12:10.620 --> 00:12:11.330
A curveball to the outside!

00:12:15.580 --> 00:12:18.090
Two hits in a row on curveballs!?

00:12:18.710 --> 00:12:21.010
One out!

00:12:21.920 --> 00:12:23.300
Th-That's strange.

00:12:23.300 --> 00:12:25.090
Next is Take... Take...

00:12:25.090 --> 00:12:26.800
Ninth batter. Pitcher: Number 1, Takenouchi.
Ready!

00:12:26.800 --> 00:12:30.220
All three pitchers pull the ball on the inside.

00:12:30.220 --> 00:12:32.890
Takenouchi isn't a particularly important batter.

00:12:32.890 --> 00:12:34.440
Let's start off with a fastball!

00:12:39.400 --> 00:12:40.530
Safe!

00:12:40.530 --> 00:12:42.320
What are you doing!?

00:12:42.320 --> 00:12:44.490
Your job isn't just leading!

00:12:45.860 --> 00:12:47.620
The fastball?

00:12:47.620 --> 00:12:50.450
When you set up for a bunt, he'll throw a fastball?

00:12:59.250 --> 00:13:00.040
Strike!

00:13:00.040 --> 00:13:01.380
He missed!?

00:13:01.380 --> 00:13:02.800
That was straight down the middle.

00:13:02.800 --> 00:13:05.050
Is he that bad?

00:13:05.050 --> 00:13:06.680
Not to mention, three bunts!?

00:13:06.680 --> 00:13:08.180
I'll try to hit this time.

00:13:09.390 --> 00:13:10.050
Here!

00:13:11.930 --> 00:13:12.850
Out!

00:13:12.850 --> 00:13:14.230
Calm down.

00:13:14.230 --> 00:13:16.480
There's a runner on second, but there are two outs.
First batter. Shortstop: Number 6, Kawashima.

00:13:16.480 --> 00:13:20.230
The first batter hit an unfamiliar
outside shootball right up the center.

00:13:20.230 --> 00:13:23.730
He went out of his way to hit it while it was 0-2 <i>because</i> it was a shootball.

00:13:23.730 --> 00:13:26.200
In that case, we'll avoid the shoot this time.

00:13:26.200 --> 00:13:27.320
A curveball.

00:13:27.320 --> 00:13:29.450
From a ball to a strike.

00:13:29.450 --> 00:13:31.780
A curve! It should go in!

00:13:31.780 --> 00:13:32.740
Shit!

00:13:32.740 --> 00:13:33.950
Go! Go!

00:13:33.950 --> 00:13:34.540
Second!

00:13:34.540 --> 00:13:35.160
Izumi!

00:13:37.120 --> 00:13:37.920
Fourth run!

00:13:37.920 --> 00:13:38.830
All right!
Fourth run!

00:13:39.500 --> 00:13:43.050
In the fertile green fields, the light shines...

00:13:43.050 --> 00:13:44.550
He still hasn't given the sign.
In the fertile green fields, the light shines...

00:13:44.550 --> 00:13:46.260
I'll hit too.
In the fertile green fields, the light shines...

00:13:46.260 --> 00:13:49.050
As long as we're far ahead,
I can play some real baseball.

00:13:49.050 --> 00:13:50.760
Second batter. Third baseman: Number 5, Ishikawa.

00:13:50.760 --> 00:13:52.010
Ready!

00:13:52.010 --> 00:13:53.390
The second batter.

00:13:53.390 --> 00:13:56.060
We ended up using the
fastball on him before, but...

00:13:56.850 --> 00:13:59.350
It's no good. Nothing's coming to mind.

00:14:01.440 --> 00:14:05.690
Strike!

00:14:05.690 --> 00:14:08.200
He just swung to the outside every time?

00:14:08.200 --> 00:14:10.070
Come on! We're just getting started!

00:14:10.070 --> 00:14:10.660
Yeah!

00:14:10.660 --> 00:14:12.030
I don't normally worry about this kind of thing...
Let's get them back!

00:14:12.030 --> 00:14:12.870
Yeah!

00:14:12.870 --> 00:14:13.740
Let's hit it!

00:14:13.740 --> 00:14:14.040
Um...

00:14:14.040 --> 00:14:15.410
Yeah!

00:14:15.410 --> 00:14:17.330
I didn't remember wrong.

00:14:17.330 --> 00:14:20.710
Everyone is hitting the opposite of what's on this table.

00:14:20.710 --> 00:14:22.790
It might be a coincidence.

00:14:22.790 --> 00:14:24.840
But if it's not,

00:14:24.840 --> 00:14:28.300
doesn't that make this data useless?

00:14:28.300 --> 00:14:31.430
Then Abe's lead...

00:14:31.430 --> 00:14:34.890
If so, then instead of being able to throw according to the lead,

00:14:34.890 --> 00:14:38.100
fastballs or left-handed pitching would be more...

00:14:38.100 --> 00:14:39.770
I don't want to step down.

00:14:39.770 --> 00:14:42.270
My predictions are off.

00:14:42.270 --> 00:14:44.570
But if I'm right?
You better get on base!

00:14:44.570 --> 00:14:45.270
Must I tickle you?

00:14:45.270 --> 00:14:46.360
If we would've benefited from me bringing this up earlier...

00:14:46.360 --> 00:14:48.780
I don't need it.

00:14:48.780 --> 00:14:51.860
If I keep giving up hits like this...

00:14:52.700 --> 00:14:54.490
I think that's a good decision.

00:14:54.490 --> 00:14:55.790
Anyway...

00:14:55.790 --> 00:14:56.870
What's wrong!?

00:14:57.830 --> 00:14:59.330
The courses are wro—

00:14:59.330 --> 00:15:01.960
Why are you crying all of a sudden!?

00:15:01.960 --> 00:15:04.000
I can't understand a damn word!

00:15:04.000 --> 00:15:06.340
He said that the courses are wrong.

00:15:06.340 --> 00:15:08.510
"The courses are wrong," right?

00:15:10.380 --> 00:15:11.260
Abe.

00:15:11.260 --> 00:15:12.970
Try listing just the courses that the batters hit.

00:15:13.930 --> 00:15:17.350
First: outside. Second: middle. Third: outside.

00:15:17.350 --> 00:15:19.730
Inside. Inside. Inside. Inside. Outside...

00:15:19.730 --> 00:15:20.810
Look.

00:15:20.810 --> 00:15:22.770
They're all hitting the opposite of their usual courses.

00:15:22.770 --> 00:15:23.560
That's true.

00:15:23.560 --> 00:15:25.060
But why?

00:15:25.060 --> 00:15:28.070
And why are you still huffing and crying!?

00:15:28.820 --> 00:15:30.240
It's nothing to cry over.

00:15:30.240 --> 00:15:32.410
We might be able to do
something about it, thanks to you.

00:15:32.410 --> 00:15:36.580
Mihashi is shaking about and Abe is yapping loudly.
Bottom of the second inning. Nishiura High to bat.

00:15:36.580 --> 00:15:39.290
I need a hit to brighten their spirits.
Fifth batter. Center fielder: Number 9, Hanai.

00:15:39.290 --> 00:15:40.250
Ready!

00:15:40.250 --> 00:15:41.000
Fifth batter. Center fielder: Number 9, Hanai.

00:15:41.000 --> 00:15:42.750
The outfielders returned to their original positions.

00:15:42.750 --> 00:15:45.420
During Izumi's and my at-bats, they moved way up.

00:15:45.420 --> 00:15:47.880
And for Sakaeguchi and Suyama, they were just slightly forward.

00:15:47.880 --> 00:15:50.130
They're not just looking at physique, are they...

00:15:50.130 --> 00:15:51.920
It really is opposite.

00:15:51.920 --> 00:15:54.390
Well, should I at least try asking?

00:15:54.390 --> 00:15:55.640
Hey.

00:15:55.640 --> 00:15:57.680
You said the courses were different,

00:15:57.680 --> 00:16:00.100
but that's not the extent of it.

00:16:00.100 --> 00:16:02.480
They won't even take a swing at their favored courses.

00:16:02.480 --> 00:16:04.150
Why do you think that is?

00:16:05.350 --> 00:16:07.820
If you don't know, just say so.

00:16:07.820 --> 00:16:08.650
Mihashi!

00:16:08.650 --> 00:16:09.940
If you know something, then spit it out!

00:16:10.900 --> 00:16:12.030
What is it?

00:16:12.900 --> 00:16:15.610
If you know, then say it! I'm next on deck...

00:16:15.610 --> 00:16:16.620
Out with it!

00:16:16.620 --> 00:16:20.490
The reason why... The answer is...

00:16:20.490 --> 00:16:21.910
Wait for it...
The reason why... The answer is...

00:16:21.910 --> 00:16:23.500
I'm waiting.

00:16:23.500 --> 00:16:27.460
They don't swing at their favored course because they're balls.

00:16:29.040 --> 00:16:31.420
They watched them pass because they were balls?

00:16:31.420 --> 00:16:34.300
I'm not having him pitch such obvious balls!

00:16:34.300 --> 00:16:37.300
Avoiding a strike and curving it into a ball is basic,

00:16:37.300 --> 00:16:39.010
and pitching to the opponent's favorite course...

00:16:41.100 --> 00:16:44.600
It's true that I have him pitch a lot of balls.

00:16:44.600 --> 00:16:46.690
Pitching a ball into the batter's favored course...

00:16:46.690 --> 00:16:48.650
has become your habit.

00:16:48.650 --> 00:16:52.440
It's not just my habit; it's one of the basics of mixing pitches!

00:16:52.440 --> 00:16:55.990
On top of that, things don't always flow according to the predetermined mix of pitches,

00:16:55.990 --> 00:16:58.070
so you think and change that part on the spot!

00:16:58.070 --> 00:16:59.450
But I've always done it that way...

00:16:59.450 --> 00:17:01.290
Now, he's...

00:17:01.290 --> 00:17:03.120
This is...

00:17:03.120 --> 00:17:04.040
Hanai!

00:17:04.040 --> 00:17:06.250
Hanai, pull it! Stick to your batting!

00:17:07.120 --> 00:17:08.540
What are you saying?

00:17:08.540 --> 00:17:11.420
I'm not a pull hitter anymore!

00:17:14.130 --> 00:17:14.970
Out!

00:17:16.090 --> 00:17:17.260
No way!

00:17:17.260 --> 00:17:19.300
That would almost always go down the center.

00:17:19.300 --> 00:17:22.430
And that relay right after, too... We're not pros, you know.

00:17:22.430 --> 00:17:26.560
It's because there were two people covering second just then.

00:17:27.390 --> 00:17:30.230
This shows that we've been closely researched.

00:17:30.230 --> 00:17:31.480
Sixth batter. First baseman: Number 3, Oki.

00:17:31.480 --> 00:17:33.110
Crap, I'm next.

00:17:33.110 --> 00:17:33.900
Abe.

00:17:33.900 --> 00:17:34.570
Yes?

00:17:34.570 --> 00:17:37.110
Think of the offense the same way you think of the defense.
Sixth batter. First baseman: Number 3, Oki.

00:17:37.110 --> 00:17:37.990
Yes, ma'am.

00:17:37.990 --> 00:17:39.240
I just told Abe that it was his habit,

00:17:39.240 --> 00:17:41.200
Nice pitch!

00:17:41.200 --> 00:17:41.740
but maybe I went a little too far.

00:17:41.740 --> 00:17:43.120
Stay calm!

00:17:43.120 --> 00:17:43.160
It's because Mihashi's the pitcher, right?

00:17:43.160 --> 00:17:44.540
Keep it going!

00:17:44.540 --> 00:17:46.250
Okay, go up!

00:17:46.910 --> 00:17:48.250
Everyone, listen up.

00:17:48.920 --> 00:17:52.750
Bijou has gathered information on us and have researched it thoroughly.

00:17:52.750 --> 00:17:55.670
They scored easily in the first two innings...

00:17:55.670 --> 00:17:57.840
because they read through
the battery's mix of pitches.

00:17:57.840 --> 00:17:58.840
The offense too.

00:17:58.840 --> 00:18:03.850
During Izumi's and Tajima's at-bats,
did you notice that the outfielders closed in?

00:18:04.680 --> 00:18:09.230
In other words, they don't
think I can pull off a big hit?

00:18:09.230 --> 00:18:14.400
During Hanai's at-bat, two fielders were positioned near second base.

00:18:14.400 --> 00:18:18.240
They knew that Hanai has quite a few hits towards the center.

00:18:18.240 --> 00:18:19.070
Listen.

00:18:19.070 --> 00:18:21.990
When it's your turn at bat, confirm the opponents' fielding positions.

00:18:21.990 --> 00:18:27.540
If they shift for you, think about why they might've done it.

00:18:27.540 --> 00:18:31.080
Try to imagine what kind of batter they think you are.

00:18:31.080 --> 00:18:34.130
On top of that, if you're confident that you can overcome their expectations,

00:18:34.130 --> 00:18:35.300
feel free to hit as usual.

00:18:35.300 --> 00:18:39.550
If you don't think your hitting power can
stomach the change, then mix up your batting.

00:18:39.550 --> 00:18:41.890
There'll definitely be an opening somewhere.

00:18:41.890 --> 00:18:43.760
Look carefully and aim for it.

00:18:43.760 --> 00:18:44.390
Yes, ma'am!

00:18:44.390 --> 00:18:45.260
All right! Let's go!

00:18:45.260 --> 00:18:48.270
I was gonna hit a grounder even if she hadn't said that.

00:18:49.020 --> 00:18:51.810
I wonder if Tajima hit that grounder because he already knew?

00:18:52.600 --> 00:18:53.650
Strike!

00:18:53.650 --> 00:18:54.480
Third.

00:18:54.480 --> 00:18:55.820
Seventh batter. Catcher: Number 2, Abe.

00:18:55.820 --> 00:18:58.280
The outfield has moved to the right.

00:18:58.280 --> 00:18:59.070
Seventh batter. Catcher: Number 2, Abe.

00:18:59.070 --> 00:18:59.990
Ready!

00:18:59.990 --> 00:19:02.160
I'm bad at letting balls go by.

00:19:02.160 --> 00:19:04.910
Since they shifted accordingly, the pitch he'll throw is...

00:19:08.080 --> 00:19:08.870
Strike!

00:19:08.870 --> 00:19:10.540
A fastball to the outside?

00:19:10.540 --> 00:19:14.840
They don't think I'm strong enough to pull balls to the outside.

00:19:17.340 --> 00:19:18.210
Strike!

00:19:18.210 --> 00:19:19.760
Cool your head.

00:19:19.760 --> 00:19:21.550
Will he try to finish me with the third pitch?

00:19:21.550 --> 00:19:25.640
If that was just for show, then now he'll drop it low and make me swing?

00:19:26.350 --> 00:19:27.100
Strike!

00:19:27.100 --> 00:19:28.100
Jeez!

00:19:28.100 --> 00:19:29.680
He struck out?

00:19:29.680 --> 00:19:30.730
Let's go!

00:19:30.730 --> 00:19:31.390
Yeah!

00:19:31.390 --> 00:19:32.190
Mihashi!

00:19:32.190 --> 00:19:33.350
Help me out with my leg guards!

00:19:36.650 --> 00:19:38.150
You don't have to carry them!

00:19:38.150 --> 00:19:40.690
I know he wants to gain time to talk, but...

00:19:40.690 --> 00:19:44.110
Abe must really be at wit's end if he's making the pitcher help him.

00:19:44.110 --> 00:19:46.870
Hey, did you realize the defense had moved?

00:19:46.870 --> 00:19:48.950
I didn't. How about you?

00:19:48.950 --> 00:19:50.370
No, me neither.

00:19:50.370 --> 00:19:52.750
But Tajima told me to pull my hit.

00:19:52.750 --> 00:19:55.580
Even though he normally
doesn't say things like that.

00:19:55.580 --> 00:19:57.460
At my first at-bat, that first pitch...

00:19:57.460 --> 00:20:01.470
I hit it, thinking it was a good one, but I was really coaxed into it.

00:20:02.010 --> 00:20:06.680
If the shift was the same, Tajima
should've gotten the same kind of pitch.

00:20:07.470 --> 00:20:11.430
You know, about Tajima...
I think he noticed Bijou's defense!

00:20:11.430 --> 00:20:12.480
Seriously?

00:20:12.480 --> 00:20:14.560
I wonder if he's always looking at the defense or whatever?

00:20:14.560 --> 00:20:16.190
Like I'd know! Ask him yourself.

00:20:16.190 --> 00:20:18.320
What are you getting angry for?

00:20:18.320 --> 00:20:20.940
I'll ask Tajima during the next offense.

00:20:20.940 --> 00:20:22.030
How frustrating.

00:20:22.030 --> 00:20:24.610
Listening to Tajima increases our chances of scoring runs.

00:20:24.610 --> 00:20:26.490
We'll get some runs in the next inning.

00:20:26.490 --> 00:20:29.870
The next inning! If I get a hit in the next inning, he's bound to forget!

00:20:29.870 --> 00:20:32.000
It's only embarrassing until then.

00:20:32.450 --> 00:20:33.330
Mihashi.

00:20:33.330 --> 00:20:34.870
From now on, shake your head.

00:20:34.870 --> 00:20:36.170
Shake?

00:20:36.170 --> 00:20:37.130
B-But...

00:20:37.130 --> 00:20:38.710
Don't worry.

00:20:38.710 --> 00:20:39.420
Listen.

00:20:39.420 --> 00:20:41.590
You're going to shake your
head according to my signs.

00:20:42.840 --> 00:20:45.590
At the end of the sign,
I'll stick out my middle finger.

00:20:45.590 --> 00:20:46.550
Like this.

00:20:46.550 --> 00:20:51.180
After that, just shake your head once.
Then, nod at the dummy sign and throw.

00:20:51.180 --> 00:20:53.350
Depending on the situation,
I might repeat it two or three times.

00:20:54.180 --> 00:20:55.890
Um... Okay.

00:20:55.890 --> 00:20:59.560
I'll shake my head and nod at the dummy.

00:20:59.560 --> 00:21:01.780
If you're finished preparing, then get moving!

00:21:01.780 --> 00:21:02.820
Yes!

00:21:02.820 --> 00:21:03.690
Keep it up!

00:21:03.690 --> 00:21:04.280
Right!

00:21:04.280 --> 00:21:06.910
A sign to shake his head on the mound?

00:21:06.910 --> 00:21:10.530
Now that I think about it, I've almost never seen Mihashi shake his head...

00:21:10.530 --> 00:21:12.740
In fact, I've <i>never</i> seen it before.

00:21:12.740 --> 00:21:14.370
Send it home!

00:21:14.370 --> 00:21:15.410
Yeah!

00:21:15.410 --> 00:21:16.000
Last!

00:21:20.420 --> 00:21:21.630
Nice throw!

00:21:21.630 --> 00:21:22.500
Hey.
Third!

00:21:22.500 --> 00:21:23.590
Abe.
Third!

00:21:23.590 --> 00:21:25.970
Don't mess up and throw by the dummy sign.

00:21:25.970 --> 00:21:28.390
Okay! I won't mess up.

00:21:28.390 --> 00:21:29.010
All right.

00:21:29.010 --> 00:21:30.300
Then...

00:21:30.300 --> 00:21:31.470
What is it?

00:21:31.470 --> 00:21:35.310
Hey. Is this... okay?

00:21:37.480 --> 00:21:39.980
Are you asking about how to shake your head?

00:21:39.980 --> 00:21:41.860
Just do it like normal!

00:21:41.860 --> 00:21:43.610
You shook your head in middle school, right?

00:21:43.610 --> 00:21:44.940
Something like how to shake your head...

00:21:45.860 --> 00:21:47.360
That's right. In middle school...

00:21:47.360 --> 00:21:48.070
Pitcher!

00:21:48.780 --> 00:21:50.490
Just now, that was fine.

00:21:51.740 --> 00:21:52.870
L-Like this?

00:21:52.870 --> 00:21:54.580
Yeah, that's perfect.

00:21:54.580 --> 00:21:55.410
I'm counting on you!

00:21:55.410 --> 00:21:57.330
Y-Y-Yeah!

00:21:57.330 --> 00:22:00.630
They didn't give him signs at Mihoshi, did they?

00:22:00.630 --> 00:22:02.790
Shaking his head wasn't
even part of the equation.

00:22:04.880 --> 00:22:06.800
Third inning! Let's shut them down!

00:22:06.800 --> 00:22:08.680
Yeah!

00:23:42.100 --> 00:23:47.070
Bijoudai Sayama High has
thoroughly researched Nishiura High.

00:23:47.070 --> 00:23:50.900
It was the first time our young team experienced such a thing.

00:23:51.860 --> 00:23:55.870
Can the bewildered Nishiura team
grasp an opening to counterattack?

00:23:56.620 --> 00:23:59.040
There's already a four-point difference in the early stages of the game.

00:23:59.040 --> 00:24:01.830
The game has just begun.

00:24:01.830 --> 00:24:03.790
Good luck, Nishiura nine!

00:24:05.290 --> 00:24:06.080
Next time:

00:24:06.080 --> 00:24:07.210
Researched.

00:24:07.210 --> 00:24:09.840
Mizutani, switch on!
